<article class="env-lemma" id="0A4Y">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0A4Y">Lemma <span data-tag="0A4Y">76.35.2</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. Let $y \in |Y|$. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is proper, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f$ is quasi-finite at all $x \in |X|$ lying over $y$ (Decent Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0ACK" data-tag="0ACK">68.18.10</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then there exists an open neighbourhood $V \subset Y$ of $y$ such that $f|_{f^{-1}(V)} : f^{-1}(V) \to V$ is finite. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/04NW" data-tag="04NW">67.34.7</a> the set of points at which $f$ is quasi-finite is an open $U \subset X$. Let $Z = X \setminus U$. Then $y \not\in f(Z)$. Since $f$ is proper the set $f(Z) \subset Y$ is closed. Choose any open neighbourhood $V \subset Y$ of $y$ with $Z \cap V = \emptyset $. Then $f^{-1}(V) \to V$ is locally quasi-finite and proper. Hence $f^{-1}(V) \to V$ is finite by Lemma <a href="/tag/0A4X" data-tag="0A4X">76.35.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>